<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teresa Teng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Teng Li-Chun, Dèng Lìjūn, Teng Li-yun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teresa Teng was a Taiwanese singer, born in Yunlin County in 1953 to parents from the mainland, who is credited as a pioneer of modern Chinese popular music. She recorded more than 1,700 songs in Mandarin, Hokkien, Cantonese, Japanese and other languages, and built a second career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1974. From the late 1970s her songs, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-moon-represents-my-heart">The Moon Represents My Heart</a>, spread through mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on cassettes; banned there for some years in the early 1980s as bourgeois, they circulated on the black market until the restrictions ended. She never performed on the mainland, supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> student protests of 1989, and died in Chiang Mai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teresa-teng.jpg" alt="Teresa Teng" width="330" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teresa_Teng_US.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
